================================================================================
NAME
       overlays-advanced - composite overlays, overlay stacks, and resonance-
                           signature interaction in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Advanced overlays extend the basic overlay system with:
           • composite overlays
           • overlay stacks
           • resonance-signature modulation
           • structural-awareness chaining
           • multi-level interpretive passes

DESCRIPTION
       Overlays are interpretive modifiers applied around triads. Advanced
       overlays extend this system by allowing overlays to be combined,
       stacked, sequenced, or fused into composite structures.

       Advanced overlays do not alter triadic structure. They alter how
       triads are interpreted, how resonance signatures are applied, and how
       structural-awareness metadata flows across cycles.

       WRSADC validates all overlay stacks before dispatch.

COMPOSITE OVERLAYS
       A composite overlay is a fused structure formed by combining two or
       more overlays into a single interpretive operator.

       Examples:
           lens ⊗ mirror
           anchor ⊗ telescope
           mirror ⊗ lens ⊗ anchor

       Composite overlays behave as a single overlay with:
           • merged resonance effects
           • unified structural-awareness metadata
           • combined interpretive behavior

       Composite overlays must satisfy overlay-schema rules.

OVERLAY STACKS
       An overlay stack is an ordered list of overlays applied sequentially.

       Example:
           [ anchor, lens, telescope ]

       Stacks differ from composites:
           • stacks preserve order
           • stacks apply overlays one at a time
           • stacks maintain separate resonance effects
           • stacks allow reversible or partial application

       Stacks are evaluated top-to-bottom:
           1. anchor stabilizes reference frame
           2. lens reframes interpretation
           3. telescope zooms into substructure

       WRSADC ensures stack safety and schema alignment.

STACK RULES
       Overlay stacks must obey:

           • no contradictory resonance signatures
           • no infinite zoom (telescope recursion)
           • no mirror inversion loops
           • no anchor conflicts
           • no schema violations

       Illegal stacks are rejected at dispatch.

RESONANCE-SIGNATURE INTERACTION
       Overlays interact with resonance signatures by modifying how operators
       propagate resonance-time.

       Each overlay has a resonance effect:

           telescope
                  amplifies or attenuates resonance gradients

           mirror
                  inverts resonance direction or polarity

           anchor
                  stabilizes resonance amplitude and prevents drift

           lens
                  refracts resonance, altering interpretive angle

       When combined:
           • composite overlays merge resonance effects
           • stacks apply resonance effects sequentially

       WRSADC computes the resulting resonance signature before dispatch.

OVERLAYS AND TRIADS
       Overlays wrap triads but do not modify triadic structure.

       Example:
           overlay_stack {
               P1 → P2 → P3
           }

       Overlays may:
           • reinterpret P1 context
           • modulate P2 resonance
           • alter P3 integration semantics

       Overlays may not:
           • skip triad phases
           • reorder triad phases
           • bypass lineage
           • bypass schemas

OVERLAYS AND LINEAGE
       Each overlay in a stack contributes lineage metadata:

           • overlay name
           • resonance effect
           • structural-awareness notes
           • composite or stack position

       Lineage ensures interpretive reproducibility.

OVERLAYS AND SCHEMAS
       Overlay behavior is governed by:

           overlay-schema
           structural-schema
           resonance-schema
           lineage-schema

       Schemas define:
           • allowed overlay combinations
           • resonance compatibility
           • stack depth limits
           • composite fusion rules

       WRSADC enforces schema compliance.

OVERLAY LIFECYCLE
       1. Author defines overlay-schema.
       2. Overlay is implemented (conceptually or as metadata).
       3. Overlay is added to a resonance package.
       4. WRSADC validates overlay or stack.
       5. Overlay wraps triads during dispatch.
       6. Resonance signatures are modulated.
       7. Lineage is updated.

NOTES
       Advanced overlays are powerful interpretive tools. They allow multi-
       level analysis, structural reframing, and resonance modulation without
       altering the underlying triadic structure.

